 the forecast far in either direction.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling and the upcoming News 12 debate could shift the race, as could fallout from the Putnam sheriff allegations against Lawler's aide.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 67 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Lawler (R) is running."]}, "overview": "New York's 17th District is a genuine battleground. Republican incumbent Mike Lawler, first elected in a Biden-won district, faces Democrat Cait Conley in a race that raters split between Tossup and Lean D. Lawler holds a clear financial edge, with $4.8 million on hand to Conley's $1.3 million, though Democratic outside groups have spent heavily to support her. Prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket, and PredictIt all price Conley around 65 percent, suggesting investors see the incumbent as vulnerable in a favorable Democratic year.\n\nThe available polling is limited and dated: a Republican-sponsored July survey gave Lawler a five-point lead, while a Democratic-sponsored poll from early July showed Conley up six. Headlines show both campaigns on offense, with Lawler attacking Conley as a carpetbagger aligned with Hochul and Mamdani, and Democrats pressing ethics allegations against his aide. Cross-party endorsements from figures like Jared Golden underscore Lawler's moderate brand. The race remains genuinely competitive, with a modest Democratic tilt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7399, "margin": 4.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7185, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6936, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7094, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6393, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6716, "margin": 3.5}]}